  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Consider $(t^2,t^2-1)$. Jimmy claims that the graph is just the graph of $y=x-1$, whereas Jenny claims that it is only the right-hand side of the graph. Determine who is correct. You can start by substituting some values of $t$ and plotting the graph.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Jenny is correct: $y=x-1$ with $x\\ge0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}x&=t^2\\ge0,\\\\y&=t^2-1\\\\y&=x-1.\\end{aligned}$$\nThus the graph is the ray on $y=x-1$ starting at $(0,-1)$ and extending to the right. Jenny is correct.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 31
